<!--l. 31--><p class="noindent" >
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
   <h3 class="likesectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-1000"></a>Abstract</h3>
<!--l. 34--><p class="noindent" >The utilization of glycerol, primary byproduct of biodiesel production, is important to enhance process
economics. In our recent prior work, it was shown that glycerol can be converted to hydrocarbon fuels
over bifunctional catalysts, containing a noble metal supported on H-ZSM-5. Over Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst,
an optimal <span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">~</span>60% yield of hydrocarbon fuels was obtained. In the present work, based on
experimental data over Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst, a lumped reaction network and kinetic model
are developed. Using differential kinetic experiments over the temperature range 300-450
<sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C, the rate constants, reaction orders and activation energies are obtained for each reaction
step. The predicted values match well with experimental data for glycerol conversion up to
<span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">~</span>90%.
<!--l. 44--><p class="noindent" >
   <h3 class="likesectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-2000"></a>Keywords</h3>
<!--l. 46--><p class="noindent" >Glycerol, Kinetics, Bifunctional Catalyst, Aromatization, Hydrodeoxygenation
<!--l. 50--><p class="indent" >
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
   <h3 class="likesectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-3000"></a>Introduction</h3>
<!--l. 54--><p class="noindent" >With the growing demand for renewable energy sources in recent years, biofuels have emerged as
promising alternatives to fossil fuels. In this context, in addition to being an energy source, biodiesel also
plays an important role in decreasing the global CO<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> footprint.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem1"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">1</span></a></sup> As reported recently, biodiesel from soy
oil can result in 57% reduction of greenhouse gases as compared to petroleum diesel, while the
corresponding decrease using waste grease is 86%..<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem2"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">2</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem3"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">3</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem4"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">4</span></a></sup>
<!--l. 62--><p class="indent" >     In biodiesel manufacture, about 10 wt% crude glycerol is generated as an undesired byproduct. The
enormous increase in global production of biodiesel has resulted in a large surplus and low price of crude
glycerol, which was about 1 cent per lb at the end of 2014.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem5"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">5</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem6"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">6</span></a></sup> To enhance biodiesel industry economics,
therefore, it is important to utilize the byproduct crude glycerol,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem7"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">7</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem8"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">8</span></a></sup> for which the purification process is
typically employed as the initial step. There are several methods for this step, including a universal
procedure described recently which can purify crude glycerol from a variety of biodiesel plants yielding
essentially pure glycerol.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem9"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">9</span></a></sup>
<!--l. 70--><p class="indent" >     Hydrocarbon fuels, including gasoline and diesel, provide high energy density and ease of
transportation, playing an important role in the current global energy supply. With its current cheap and
ready availability, conversion of glycerol to hydrocarbon (GTH) fuels would increase the
economic value of glycerol and provide an additional bioenergy source. In our recent work,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem10"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">10</span></a></sup>
bifunctional catalysts (Pt/H-ZSM-5 and Pd/H-ZSM-5) were selected, prepared, characterized, and
tested for GTH conversion. Under optimal conditions, about 90% glycerol conversion and 60%
yield of aromatic hydrocarbons were achieved over Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst. A mechanism of
sequential hydrodeoxygenation and aromatization was proposed for GTH conversion. Similar
works, using catalysts such as PdO/H-ZSM-5, Zn/H-ZSM-5 and Sn/H-ZSM-5, have also been
reported.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem11"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">11</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem12"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">12</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem13"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">13</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem14"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">14</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem15"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">15</span></a></sup>
<!--l. 78--><p class="indent" >     The GTH conversion is a complex process, involving more than 20 species, such as acetaldehyde,
propenal (acrolein), hydroxyacetone, ethylene and C<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">6</span></sub>-C<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">8</span></sub> aromatic hydrocarbons. The reaction
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
pathways, including dehydration, dehydrogenation/hydrogenation and oligomerization, have
been proposed.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem14"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">14</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem15"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">15</span></a></sup> To our knowledge, however, kinetic studies of GTH conversion have not
been reported previously. For such complex reaction networks, containing a large number
of chemical species, lumped kinetic models are typically developed to represent the overall
reaction pathways,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem16"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">16</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem17"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">17</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem18"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">18</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem19"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">19</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem20"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">20</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem21"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">21</span></a></sup> which can be further used for reactor design and process
scale-up. In the present study, based on our prior work,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem10"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">10</span></a></sup> differential kinetic experiments of GTH
conversion are reported, a lumped kinetic model is developed, and reaction mechanism is
discussed.
<!--l. 91--><p class="noindent" >
     <h3 class="likesectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-4000"></a>Experimental</h3>
<!--l. 93--><p class="noindent" >
     <h4 class="likesubsectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-5000"></a>Materials, Catalyst Preparation and Characterization</h4>
<!--l. 95--><p class="noindent" >Palladium (II) nitrate hydrate (99.8% metals basis), from Alfa Aesar, was used as the Pd metal
precursor. ZSM-5 (Si/Al=40) of ammonium form was supplied by Zeolyst International. Pure
glycerol (ACS grade) and 37 wt% HCl were obtained from Mallinckrodt Chemicals. The
chemical standards, including acetaldehyde, benzene, cumene, ethylene, hydroxyacetone,
propanal, acrolein, propylene, toluene, m-xylene, o-xylene and p-xylene, were from Sigma
Aldrich. Ultrahigh purity grade gases (H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>, He, O<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>, N<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> etc.) were purchased from Indiana
Oxygen.
<!--l. 102--><p class="indent" >     The ammonium form of ZSM-5 (Si/Al=40) was converted to its H-form (H-ZSM-5) by
calcining in air at 500 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C for 4 hours.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem22"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">22</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem23"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">23</span></a></sup> The catalyst was prepared by impregnation method.
Briefly, Pd precursor was dissolved in deionized water, and added dropwise to the well stirred
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
H-ZSM-5 slurry, with pH=5.6 and stirring continued for at least 8 hours at room temperature.
The slurry was then rinsed and dried in air at 100 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C. The Pd loading was 5 wt%. In later
sections, metal loading and Si/Al ratio in H-ZSM-5 are not noted explicitly when describing the
catalyst. Thus, Pd/H-ZSM-5 refers to 5 wt% Pd loaded on H-form of ZSM-5 with a Si/Al ratio of
40.
<!--l. 111--><p class="indent" >     Since the same Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalyst, as in our prior publication,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem10"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">10</span></a></sup> was used in the present work,
results of catalyst characterization, including BET specific surface area, mean pore diameter, Pd metal
dispersion (by H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>-O<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> titration<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem24"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">24</span></a></sup>), Pd particle size, AEC-ICP element analysis, NH<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">3</span></sub>-TPD, transmission
electron microscopy (TEM), X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) and X-ray powder diffraction
(XRD), can be found in that publication and its Supplementary Information. Briefly, the high BET surface
area (437 m<sup><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sup>/g) and moderate mean pore diameter (about 0.5 nm) of Pd/H-ZSM-5 indicates good
accessibility of reactants (e.g. glycerol, acrolein and olefin gases) over Pd/H-ZSM-5 catalysts. Both
surface area and mean pore diameter data are consistent with those reported in the literature.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem25"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">25</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem26"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">26</span></a></sup> The Pd
particle sizes provided by TEM (3.8 nm) and H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>-O<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> titration (3.2 nm) techniques are consistent,
corresponding to Pd metal dispersion of 23-30%. The XRD patterns for Pd/H-ZSM-5 are
similar to unsupported MFI (ZSM-5), as reported in the International Zeolite Association (IZA)
structure database.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem27"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">27</span></a></sup> No Pd peaks were identified, likely due to high metal dispersions and low
metal loading.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem28"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">28</span></a></sup> The NH<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">3</span></sub>-TPD measurements showed a Brønsted acid peak at about 650
K.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem23"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">23</span></a></sup>
<!--l. 122--><p class="noindent" >
     <h4 class="likesubsectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-6000"></a>Kinetic Measurements and Product Analysis</h4>
<!--l. 124--><p class="noindent" >The experiments were conducted in a continuous fixed-bed reactor system, described in our prior work.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem10"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">10</span></a></sup>
The catalysts were packed in a stainless steel fixed-bed reactor (with internal diameter 10 mm). Prior to
kinetic measurements, catalysts were activated at 400 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C, 1 atm for 4 h under a gas mixture (25% H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>,
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
75% N<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>) flow. Feedstocks were heated to their boiling points to ensure evaporation prior to
being pumped by an IP 25 Isocratic pump from Dionex into the reactor. The product mixture
was condensed in a dual-wall glass condenser, cooled by propanediol circulation (to -20 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C)
controlled by a circulator (Thermo Haake C10). The liquid products were collected every 10
min and then analyzed, while the gaseous products were analyzed on-line every 5 min. The
standard operating conditions were 400 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C, 1 atm total pressure, 0.05 g catalyst, mixed gas of
N<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> and H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> at flow rates 100 mL/min and 50 mL/min respectively, and pure glycerol feed
rate 0.6 mL/h (liquid, at room temperature). Based on the standard operating procedure, to
obtain kinetic data under various conditions, parameters including temperature, reactant feed
flow, catalyst packing amount were varied. All experiments were conducted under excess
hydrogen flow (feed molar ratio of H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> to glycerol <span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">~ </span>10). A blank test with packing inert material
(amorphous SiO<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub>), instead of Pd/H-ZSM-5, was conducted under the standard reaction conditions. It
was found that glycerol conversion was <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003C; </span>1%. All experiments have mass balances of 90 <span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">±</span>
3%, consistent with the literature.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem29"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">29</span></a></sup> Possible factors affecting mass balance include liquid
hold-up in the reactor, condenser and tubing. All kinetics measurements were conducted under
differential conditions, corresponding to 5-10 % conversion. All experiments were repeated at least
twice.
<!--l. 146--><p class="indent" >     The products from GTH process split into three phases: non-condensable gaseous, upper organic
liquid and lower aqueous liquid, which were analyzed by three different instruments. The gases were
analyzed by a micro-GC (Agilent 3000A) equipped with a thermal conductivity detector, a Mol-Sieve 5A
column and a Plot U column. The organic liquid products were analyzed by an Agilent GC 6890 equipped
with flame ionization detector (FID) and a DB-WAX (30 m <span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">&#x00D7; </span>0.32 mm) column, while the aqueous liquid
samples were analyzed by an Agilent GC 5890 equipped with a FID and Select Biodiesel for glycerides
ultiMetal Column (15m <span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">&#x00D7; </span>0.32 mm, ID=0.10 mm) and a retention gap. Calibration was performed for all
the components.
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
<!--l. 154--><p class="noindent" >
     <h3 class="likesectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-7000"></a>Results and Discussion</h3>
<!--l. 156--><p class="noindent" >
     <h4 class="likesubsectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-8000"></a>Proposed Reaction Pathways</h4>
<!--l. 158--><p class="noindent" >As described in our prior work,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem10"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">10</span></a></sup> oxygenates (acrolein, acetol, etc.) were primary products when
unsupported H-ZSM-5, Pd/SiO<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> (amorphous) and Pt/SiO<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> (amorphous) were used as catalysts, although
the use of H-ZSM-5 promoted the formation of target products, aromatic hydrocarbon fuels. The addition
of noble metal Pd to H-ZSM-5, as compared to unsupported H-ZSM-5, enhanced yield towards aromatics
and decreased the selectivity to oxygenates (see <a 
href="#x1-16008r1">Table&#x00A0;1<!--tex4ht:ref: T_products --></a>), where selectivity of a component refers to the
production rate of that component per unit consumption rate of glycerol. In our prior work, the metal
and acidity of ZSM-5 effects were discussed. Briefly, it was concluded that GTH conversion
follows sequential hydrodeoxygenation (HDO) and aromatization steps, promoted by metals
(Pd or Pt) and acidic H-ZSM-5 support. Thus, surface Pd and acidic sites of H-ZSM-5 are
proposed as the active sites. The operating conditions, including temperature, H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> partial pressure,
glycerol feed flow rate and catalyst packed amount, were also varied and optimized in our
prior work. The optimized parameters were used as the standard conditions in the present
work.
<!--l. 172--><p class="indent" >     Since the only carbon containing species in the feed was glycerol, all three carbon containing
products (oxygenates, gases and aromatics) derived from it. As shown in <a 
href="#x1-16001r1">Figure&#x00A0;1<!--tex4ht:ref: F_network --></a>, the partially
deoxygenated products, oxygenates, are proposed as intermediates resulting in the fully deoxygenated
products - gases and aromatics, as also suggested by others.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem14"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">14</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem15"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">15</span></a></sup> This leads to the proposed steps R1, R2
and R3. Further, as reported in the literature,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem30"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">30</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem31"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">31</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem32"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">32</span></a></sup> gases can be converted over ZSM-5 type zeolites to
aromatics, thus step R4 is included in the reaction network. In summary, oxygenates are first formed from
glycerol (step R1), followed by formation of aromatics either directly (step R3) or indirectly via gases
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
(steps R2 and R4).
<!--l. 182--><p class="noindent" >
     <h4 class="likesubsectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-9000"></a>Absence of Mass and Heat Transfer Effects</h4>
<!--l. 184--><p class="noindent" >Before conducting the kinetic measurements, using well known procedures, the plug flow condition was
confirmed by satisfying the criteria proposed previously.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem33"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">33</span></a></sup> Specifically, the reactor diameter is more than
10 times the catalyst particle diameter, while the catalyst bed height is more than 50 times the catalyst
particle diameter. The absence of internal/external mass transfer limitations was also confirmed
(<a 
href="#x1-16002r2">Figure&#x00A0;2<!--tex4ht:ref: F_transfer --></a>). By varying only packed catalyst particle diameter (<span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">d</span><sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">p</span></sub>) in the range 100 micron <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003C; d</span><sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">p</span></sub> <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003C; </span>1000
micron, <a 
href="#x1-16002r2">Figure&#x00A0;2<!--tex4ht:ref: F_transfer --></a>(a) was obtained. It shows that when <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">d</span><sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">p</span></sub> <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003C; </span>150 micron (corresponding to 100 mesh),
internal mass transfer effect was negligible. <a 
href="#x1-16002r2">Figure&#x00A0;2<!--tex4ht:ref: F_transfer --></a>(b) was obtained by varying feed flow
rate, while keeping other operating conditions, including contact time (W/F), unchanged. It
indicates that for feed flow rate <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003E; </span>0.4 mL/h, no significant external mass transfer effect exists.
Thus, <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">d</span><sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">p</span></sub> <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003C; </span>150 micron and feed flow rate <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">&#x003E; </span>0.4 mL/h were used in the kinetic experiments.
Further, the criteria by Weisz and Prater<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem34"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">34</span></a></sup> <img 
src="Kinetics_CH40x.png" alt="(  2        )
  dCpriD&#x03C1;cat&#x003C; 1
   i eff"  class="left" align="middle"> and Mears<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem35"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">35</span></a></sup> <img 
src="Kinetics_CH41x.png" alt="(                  )
 ri&#x0394;Hi&#x03C1;catd2pEai &#x003C; 0.15
     hRT"  class="left" align="middle">
were both satisfied as well, confirming the absence of mass and heat transfer effects in all
measurements.
<!--l. 201--><p class="noindent" >
     <h4 class="likesubsectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-10000"></a>Kinetic Model Development</h4>
<!--l. 203--><p class="noindent" >In the reaction pathways shown in <a 
href="#x1-16001r1">Figure&#x00A0;1<!--tex4ht:ref: F_network --></a>, each step (R1-R4) contains more than one elementary
reaction. For example, step R1, when converting glycerol to acrolein, involves two sequential
dehydration reactions,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem36"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">36</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem37"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">37</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem38"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">38</span></a></sup> while more complex reactions (e.g. hydrogenation, dehydrogenation,
deoxygenation, etc.) occur when other oxygenates are produced.<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem14"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">14</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem15"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">15</span></a><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">,</span><a 
href="#bibitem39"><span 
