     <h4 class="likesubsectionHead"><a 
 id="x1-11000"></a>Glycerol to Oxygenates (Step R1)</h4>
<!--l. 259--><p class="noindent" >Since excess H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> (molar ratio of H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> to glycerol <span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-120">~</span>10) is used in all cases, H<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7t-x-x-90">2</span></sub> pressure effect was considered
as a constant contribution to the reaction rates. Kinetic measurements were carried out at various
temperatures in the range 300-450 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C. Although only results at 400 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C are shown below, since it gives
optimum aromatic yields,<sup class="textsuperscript"><a 
href="#bibitem10"><span 
class="ptmr8t-x-x-109">10</span></a></sup> fitting results for other temperatures gave essentially the same
conclusions.
<!--l. 265--><p class="indent" >     When glycerol was fed as reactant, under differential operating conditions, all lumped components
(oxygenates, gases and aromatics) were detected. By varying glycerol feed flow rate, its consumption
rate corresponding to its average bed partial pressure, was measured, as shown and fitted in
<a 
href="#x1-16003r3">Figure&#x00A0;3<!--tex4ht:ref: F_glycerol --></a>.
<!--l. 272--><p class="indent" >     Being the first step of the network, the consumption rate of glycerol depends only on glycerol partial
pressure. Since a linear fit works well, step R1 appears to be first-order, which is consistent with the
