same when other temperatures (300-450 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C) are used. Reaction activation energies (<span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">E</span><sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">a</span></sub>) of steps R1-R4
were calculated using the Arrhenius equation (<a 
href="#x1-13001r16">16<!--tex4ht:ref: Eq31 --></a>). With the reaction orders remaining the same, data for
the other temperatures (300-450 <sup><span 
class="zptmcm7y-x-x-90">&#x2218;</span></sup>C) were used to obtain the corresponding <span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-120">k</span><sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">i</span></sub> values. The reaction
                                                                                         
                                                                                         
activation energies (E<sub><span 
class="zptmcm7m-x-x-90">a</span></sub>) for steps (R1-R4) were then calculated by linear fits using the Arrhenius equation
(Eq. <a 
href="#x1-13001r16">16<!--tex4ht:ref: Eq31 --></a>). Good linearity was obtained for all four steps, as shown in <a 
href="#x1-16006r6">Figure&#x00A0;6<!--tex4ht:ref: Fitting --></a>, and the fitted results are
shown in <a 
href="#x1-16010r3">Table&#x00A0;3<!--tex4ht:ref: T_Ea --></a>.
